   397	
   398	### 5.4c `prior_record` is ambiguous, and it matters exactly where it hurts
   399	
   400	Running the decision function in shadow mode against the real callers — it
   401	observes and records, it does not act — produced 17 agreements, 2
   402	divergences and 10 rows the caller never reached. One divergence is a model
   403	defect, not an implementation defect.
   404	
   405	`prior_record` is defined as "what the pre-run baseline manifest says". There
   406	are **two** such manifests and the definition does not choose between them:
   407	
   408	- the **raw** file on disk, and
   409	- the **sanitized** one the loader produces, which drops every record whose
   410	  relpath traverses a symlinked component.
   411	
   412	They agree everywhere except on the symlink-traversal rows — which are the
   413	security-critical ones, and the same rows §5.8 is about. An observer reading
   414	the sanitized manifest sees `none` and concludes `PRESERVE_UNOWNED`; an
   415	observer reading the raw file sees `hash` and concludes `OMIT_RECORD`. Both
   416	are defensible readings of a dimension that never said which it meant.
   417	
   418	The resolution is not to pick the more convenient one. The **sanitized**
   419	manifest is the authority, because honouring a record whose path crosses a
   420	symlink is precisely what the ADR-155 decision-(v) provenance fence exists
   421	to prevent — but the definition in §2.2 has to say so, and the harness has
   422	to observe the same thing the caller does, or the two instruments will keep
   423	disagreeing about cells neither of them is wrong about.
   424	
   425	This is the kind of defect that survives eleven rounds of code review: every
   426	branch reads *a* manifest, each reads a defensible one, and no branch is
   427	individually wrong.
   428	
   429	### 5.4d The missing cell was the most important one
   430	
   431	The table had nine `protocol` rows and none for the combination that matters
   432	most: an **adopter-customised pointer on a normal (maintainer) upgrade**.
   433	`OWN-0072` covers the same content under `ceremony=user`; the ordinary path
   434	was simply absent.
   435	
   436	Deriving its expected pair exposed a **data-loss defect in the proposed
   437	decision function**. For that cell the function returned `REFRESH` — it would
   438	have overwritten a customised root `PROTOCOL.md`. That is the verified S238
   439	loss that ADR-155 decision (iii) exists to close, and the live code has
   440	preserved it correctly all along.
   441	
   442	The asymmetry the function was missing is deliberate and is now stated in it:
   443	
   444	| Surface | An adopter edit is… | Because |
   445	|---|---|---|
   446	| `SPEC/v1` | a **fork of the contract** → forced refresh | it is the published compliance contract (ADR-155-AMEND-1 §4) |
   447	| `PROTOCOL.md` | **adopter content** → preserved | overwriting it is the verified S238 loss (ADR-155 (iii)) |
   448	
   449	Both record a **canonical** digest regardless, because recording the
   450	customised bytes would make the *next* upgrade read `H_dst == H_base` and
   451	clobber them — the C.5 idempotency trap.
   452	
   453	Two things are worth separating here. The defect was in the **new** code, not
   454	the old: a refactor that had been driven only by "keep the map green" would
   455	have shipped it, because **no existing row covered the cell**. What found it
   456	was asking the completeness question — *which combinations does this surface
   457	have, and is each one present?* — which is the one question a per-branch
   458	review never asks.
   459	
   460	### 5.4e Install and upgrade generate DIFFERENT pointers — every upgrade breaks it (CLOSED by PLAN-168 W2 — INV-4; discovery record below)
   461	
   462	Chasing a `HASH_UNCLASSIFIED` observation led to a defect in the live code
   463	that has nothing to do with ownership records.
   464	
   465	`install.sh` writes the root pointer and then **substitutes** its
   466	placeholders, so the adopter gets a real path. `upgrade.sh` regenerates the
   467	same file from a heredoc that leaves `{{PROTOCOL_SOURCE}}` **literal**, and
   468	nothing substitutes it afterwards. Verified against the live tree:
   469	
   470	| after | literal `{{PROTOCOL_SOURCE}}` occurrences |
     1	#!/usr/bin/env bash
     2	# =============================================================================
     3	# PLAN-168 W1 (AC-5) — the ownership nightly GATE.
     4	#
     5	# Runs the ownership e2e harness and compares the observed RED id set
     6	# against scripts/tests/ownership-expected-reds.txt. ANY set difference fails
     7	# — including shrinkage: deliberate reds are part of the contract
     8	# (CLAUDE.md §4), and an all-green run means the truth table changed, which
     9	# is a reason to STOP, not to celebrate.
    10	#
    11	# This is a separate script (not inline YAML) so it can be exercised by a
    12	# positive control (test-ownership-nightly-gate.sh) — a gate nobody can test
    13	# is a gate nobody has proven (PLAN-167: 8 instrument defects; PLAN-168
    14	# debate r1 QA must-fix 2: describing behavior is not a gate).
    15	#
    16	# rc semantics, explicit by design (codex rail r1 P1):
    17	#   - harness rc >= 2  => harness/infra error, NEVER comparable => gate FAILS
    18	#   - summary line must exist and report HARNESS-ERR=0 (partial output fails)
    19	#   - non-empty expected set REQUIRES harness rc == 1 (its designed status)
    20	#   - empty     expected set REQUIRES harness rc == 0
    21	#   - observed RED set must equal the expected set exactly
    22	#   - any OTHER non-GREEN status (TIMEOUT / ESCAPE / AMBIG) fails OUTRIGHT
    23	#     (codex rail r2 P1): an expected-red id that starts timing out or
    24	#     escaping the target keeps the id set unchanged — comparing ids alone
    25	#     would wave a MORE SEVERE regression through as "same set".
    26	#
    27	# NEVER wire the harness's --map mode into this gate: --map exits 0 over
    28	# failures by design (reporting mode) — a dead gate by construction.
    29	#
    30	# Test seams (positive control only — CI uses the defaults):
    31	#   OWNERSHIP_GATE_HARNESS       command to run instead of the real harness
    32	#   OWNERSHIP_GATE_EXPECTED      expected-reds file to compare against
    33	#   OWNERSHIP_GATE_EXPECTED_ALL  full-id-set file (default: harness --list)
    34	#
    35	# Exit: 0 = set stable. 1 = gate failed (set changed / harness error / vacuous
    36	#       output). 2 = gate usage/infra error (missing expected file).
    37	# =============================================================================
    38	set -uo pipefail
    39	
    40	SCRIPT_DIR="$( cd "$( dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" )" && pwd )"
    41	REPO_ROOT="$( cd "$SCRIPT_DIR/../.." && pwd )"
    42	
    43	HARNESS="${OWNERSHIP_GATE_HARNESS:-}"
    44	if [[ -z "$HARNESS" ]]; then
    45	  HARNESS="bash '$SCRIPT_DIR/test-ownership-table.sh'"
    46	fi
    47	EXPECTED="${OWNERSHIP_GATE_EXPECTED:-$SCRIPT_DIR/ownership-expected-reds.txt}"
    48	
    49	[[ -f "$EXPECTED" ]] || { echo "GATE-ERR: expected-reds file not found: $EXPECTED" >&2; exit 2; }
    50	
    51	WORK="$( mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/own-gate.XXXXXX" )" || exit 2
    52	trap 'rm -rf "$WORK" 2>/dev/null || true' EXIT
    53	
    54	MAP="$WORK/map.txt"
    55	ERRS="$WORK/err.txt"
    56	
    57	rc=0
    58	( cd "$REPO_ROOT" && eval "$HARNESS" ) > "$MAP" 2> "$ERRS" || rc=$?
    59	
    60	cat "$MAP"
    61	sed -n '1,40p' "$ERRS" >&2 || true
    62	
    63	if [[ "$rc" -ge 2 ]]; then
    64	  echo "GATE-RED: harness returned rc=$rc (harness/infra error — not comparable)" >&2
    65	  exit 1
    66	fi
    67	
    68	# The summary line is load-bearing: without it, a run that died mid-table
    69	# would present a truncated (smaller) non-GREEN set that could still match a
    70	# shrunken expectation. HARNESS-ERR must be literally 0.
    71	if ! grep -E '^GREEN=[0-9]+[[:space:]]+RED=[0-9]+[[:space:]]+AMBIG=[0-9]+[[:space:]]+HARNESS-ERR=0$' "$MAP" >/dev/null; then
    72	  echo "GATE-RED: summary line missing or HARNESS-ERR>0 — partial or vacuous output cannot pass" >&2
    73	  exit 1
    74	fi
    75	
    76	# The RED-set comparison alone cannot prove the FULL table ran: a run that
    77	# silently skipped every GREEN cell would still present the expected REDs and
    78	# an honest-looking summary (codex pack-review P1). Demand that the observed
    79	# id set equals the TABLE's id set — authority: the harness's own --list
    80	# (which reads the TSV), overridable only by the positive control's seam.
    81	if [[ -n "${OWNERSHIP_GATE_EXPECTED_ALL:-}" ]]; then
    82	  grep -E '^OWN-' "$OWNERSHIP_GATE_EXPECTED_ALL" | LC_ALL=C sort > "$WORK/all-exp.txt"
    83	else
    84	  ( cd "$REPO_ROOT" && bash scripts/tests/test-ownership-table.sh --list ) 2>/dev/null \
    85	    | awk '{print $1}' | grep -E '^OWN-' | LC_ALL=C sort > "$WORK/all-exp.txt"
    86	fi
    87	grep -E '^OWN-[0-9]+[[:space:]]' "$MAP" | awk '{print $1}' | LC_ALL=C sort > "$WORK/all-got.txt"
    88	if [[ ! -s "$WORK/all-exp.txt" ]]; then
    89	  echo "GATE-RED: could not derive the full table id set (--list empty) — cannot certify coverage" >&2
    90	  exit 1
    91	fi
    92	if ! diff -u "$WORK/all-exp.txt" "$WORK/all-got.txt" >/dev/null; then
    93	  echo "GATE-RED: observed cell set != FULL table — a partial run certifies nothing:" >&2
    94	  diff -u "$WORK/all-exp.txt" "$WORK/all-got.txt" | grep -E '^[+-]OWN' | head -10 >&2
    95	  exit 1
    96	fi
    97	
    98	# Statuses other than GREEN and RED are never expected and never comparable:
    99	# TIMEOUT / ESCAPE / AMBIG on an EXPECTED-red id would keep the id set intact
   100	# while hiding a more severe regression behind "same set" (codex rail r2 P1).
   101	grep -E '^OWN-[0-9]+[[:space:]]' "$MAP" \
   102	  | awk '$2 != "GREEN" && $2 != "RED" {print $1" "$2}' > "$WORK/other.txt"
   103	if [[ -s "$WORK/other.txt" ]]; then
   104	  echo "GATE-RED: cell(s) in a status that is never acceptable (TIMEOUT/ESCAPE/AMBIG):" >&2
   105	  sed 's/^/  /' "$WORK/other.txt" >&2
   106	  exit 1
   107	fi
   108	
   109	grep -E '^OWN-[0-9]+[[:space:]]' "$MAP" \
   110	  | awk '$2 == "RED" {print $1}' | LC_ALL=C sort > "$WORK/got.txt"
   111	grep -E '^OWN-' "$EXPECTED" | LC_ALL=C sort > "$WORK/exp.txt"
   112	
   113	if ! diff -u "$WORK/exp.txt" "$WORK/got.txt"; then
   114	  echo "GATE-RED: the RED set CHANGED (shrinkage included: all-green means the table changed — stop and find out why)" >&2
   115	  exit 1
   116	fi
   117	
   118	if [[ -s "$WORK/exp.txt" && "$rc" -ne 1 ]]; then
   119	  echo "GATE-RED: rc=$rc but the expected set is non-empty (harness must exit 1 over expected reds)" >&2
   120	  exit 1
   121	fi
   122	if [[ ! -s "$WORK/exp.txt" && "$rc" -ne 0 ]]; then
   123	  echo "GATE-RED: rc=$rc but the expected set is empty (harness must exit 0 when everything is green)" >&2
   124	  exit 1
   125	fi
   126	
   127	echo "ownership gate: RED set stable ($(wc -l < "$WORK/exp.txt" | tr -d ' ') expected RED cells, zero TIMEOUT/ESCAPE/AMBIG)"
   128	exit 0

